Your app is already enrolled. Use this page when you change application code, update the shared workflow, or need a different template for the existing Bitrise app.

**Example throughout this page — MyChickenapp.** Its application code and its enrollment YAML live in separate repositories. The app already has a Bitrise identity and an applied shared workflow.

| Question | Where to look |
|---|---|
| Which application code will be built? | The application repository, branch, and source revision selected for the run. |
| Which commands will execute? | The workflow configuration currently applied to the Bitrise app. |
| What starts the run? | An enabled trigger matching the received event, or an approved manual/API/scheduled start. |
| Who changes the build commands? | Platform Engineering, through shared template review and adoption. |

An application commit and a template version identify two different inputs to a build. Record both when investigating its result.

<Aside type="caution" title="Keep the existing enrollment and Bitrise app">

App developers maintain code, tests, and `campfile.yml` in the app repo. Platform Engineering maintains the shared `bitrise.yml`. Accepted enrollment requests are create-only. Use the approved existing-app change procedure for template or lifecycle changes.

</Aside>

## 1. Choose the change you are making

| Your change | Where the change belongs | What starts the next action |
|---|---|---|
| Change Login, Home, or another feature | Application repo. | A configured app-repo event reaches Bitrise and matches a trigger. |
| Push a second fix to an open application PR | Same application repo and PR branch. | The configured PR-update event can start checks for the new revision. |
| Change tests or app dependencies | Application repo, including the applicable lockfile. | The configured app trigger starts the required checks and build. |
| Change a shared build command, test stilstep, or deployment script | Bitrise template repo, maintained by Platform Engineering. | Catalog validation, review, publication, then approved adoption by the consumer. |
| Upgrade the template version | Approved existing-app adoption procedure. | The platform applies the approved version to the existing app. |
| Change the template ID | Approved existing-app lifecycle or migration procedure. | The platform verifies compatibility and applies the approved configuration. |
| Investigate an unsuccessful run | Existing Bitrise app and destination evidence. | Follow reviewed recovery before repeating operations with external side effects. |

## 2. Understand the two repository events

| Event | System that processes it | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| First enrollment request reaches protected `main` in `mobile-platform-configuration` | Vela and the configuration engine. | Provisions the Bitrise app and applies its selected workflow configuration. |
| Later application PR or push event | The configured GitHub-to-Bitrise connection and Bitrise trigger rules. | Selects a workflow in the existing Bitrise app to build the intended application revision. |

The first setup records the relationship between the app repository, Bitrise app, and selected template. Later source builds reuse that relationship.

### What remains available after the first enrollment?

After successful enrollment, the platform has an existing Bitrise app connected to the application source and configured with the selected workflow. That connection and applied configuration are what later builds use.

You can follow the next build without reopening the original enrollment request. Open the existing Bitrise app and inspect its incoming event, selected workflow, and checkout revision.

### How GitHub tells Bitrise about a change

1. You push a commit or open/update a PR in the **application repository**.
2. The configured GitHub connection sends the event to Bitrise. A webhook is an event notification that contains details such as the repository, event, and revision.
3. Bitrise evaluates the configured event and branch filters.
4. A matching trigger selects the workflow or pipeline.
5. The run checks out its selected revision and executes the applied workflow configuration.
6. Inspect the build log and configured GitHub check/status to confirm the result belongs to that revision.

For a webhook-based integration, the platform owner can inspect the application repo's **Settings → Webhooks → Recent deliveries**, subject to GitHub access and interface labels. A GitHub App integration uses installation event subscriptions, which are managed separately. Confirm which connection your enterprise uses before diagnosing a missing event. [Bitrise incoming webhooks](https://docs.bitrise.io/en/bitrise-platform/integrations/webhooks/adding-incoming-webhooks), [GitHub App events](https://docs.bitrise.io/en/bitrise-platform/repository-access/github-app-integration)

The supplied v1.10.0 template defines workflows but supplies no complete app trigger mapping. Record the actual PR event, source/target branch filters, and selected workflow with Platform Engineering. The branch and workflow examples below illustrate the flow.

