## 6. Case D — upgrade the version or change the template ID

The following values illustrate a request to change workflow selection. They are a discussion record for the platform owner, not an enrollment update schema.

```text
Existing Bitrise app: [recorded app identity]
Application source revision: [commit selected for verification]
Current template ID and version: [actual applied values]
Requested template ID and version: [approved compatible selection]
Reason for the change: [required behavior]
Catalog commit: [reviewed published revision]
Adoption procedure: [owner-confirmed process]
Verification routes: [applicable workflows]
Recovery procedure: [reviewed procedure]
```

| Change | What the owner must establish |
|---|---|
| Same template ID, successor version | Input compatibility, changed steps, required bindings, adoption procedure, and verification. |
| Different template ID | Supported app platform, source branch, workflow names, package producers, protected bindings, destinations, and existing-app migration support. |
| Add a new workflow requirement | Publish/adopt a version that implements the requirement, then verify its result in the existing app. |
| Change the source branch or connected repository | Use the approved lifecycle procedure and verify the new event filters and checkout identity. |

The accepted request is create-only. The supplied references omit the existing-app update manifest/API and migration commands. Have the platform owner supply that procedure and record its result. Preserve the existing app and destination identities throughout the change.

### Example — choose a different template ID

Suppose MyChickenapp needs a different approved workflow family. Give the platform owner the current app identity, current template ID/version, and proposed ID/version. The owner checks that the new family supports the app's native targets, workflow names, signing, and destinations, then performs the supported migration.

After migration, inspect the applied workflow in the existing Bitrise app and run the approved verification revision. Confirm the intended trigger selects an available workflow and that the produced candidate reaches the correct destination when delivery is permitted.

## 7. Case E — start another build or investigate a failed run

A manual or scheduled run follows the platform's configured start mechanism. Confirm the selected app revision, applied workflow, runtime inputs, and permitted delivery effects before starting it. A manual run does not demonstrate that webhook triggering works.

If a run failed before a permitted retry, follow the reviewed recovery procedure. If it may already have uploaded, signed, enabled, or distributed a candidate, preserve the build ID and let the owner reconcile exact destination state first.

## 8. Follow the candidate from build to delivery

For each applicable Android and iOS route, record the source commit, build ID, package version, artifact hash/reference, applied template version, and required results.

| Stage | What to inspect |
|---|---|
| Tests and coverage | Required suites actually executed and fresh reports passed the configured thresholds. |
| NowSecure, when included | Completed assessment for the exact binary and approved severity decision. |
| Signing | Required native signing and destination processing are confirmed. |
| Private Bitrise artifact | Exact APK, IPA, or other expected output is available in the run. |
| Destination delivery | Intended version is enabled/processed and available to the approved audience. |

The supplied v1.10.0 example can upload private Bitrise artifacts while `SKIP_DEPLOY` remains `true`. Its `qa_parallel` and `uat_parallel` workflows launch native platform builds. Inspect the selected version for actual tests and security steps. [Bitrise Pipelines](/mobile/cicd/bitrise-pipelines/)

When the platform uses a separate recurring-distribution worker, confirm how it discovers the existing Bitrise app's builds and records progress. The source describes inventory, a cursor, version markers, and checksums. It omits the complete worker, schedule, and concurrency contract.

| Reported stream outcome | Required handling |
|---|---|
| `matched` or `completed` | Verify the marked destination version and preserve completed operations. |
| `not_published` before mutation | Correct the deterministic input/retention issue while preserving cursor and evidence. |
| `side_effect_unknown` | Stop further writes and have the owner reconcile the exact version marker and checksum. |
| Identity, cursor, pagination, or latest-version mismatch | Have the authorized owner disable the external schedule and investigate. |
| Missing or duplicate inventory | Keep the schedule disabled and correct inventory through review. |

