## 9. Trace a missing, duplicate, or unexpected build

Follow the chain in order. Begin in the repo where the change was made, then inspect event delivery, trigger selection, the Bitrise run, and destination results.

| Observation | Follow the chain |
|---|---|
| No build after a push or PR update | Confirm the event occurred in the app repo, the connection delivered it, and an enabled event/branch filter matched. |
| Connection delivered the event but no run appeared | Ask the owner to inspect trigger filters and selected workflow availability. |
| One action produced multiple builds | Compare event types and run causes. PR and push triggers may both match. Have the owner check duplicate connections and intended trigger overlap. |
| A run used an unexpected revision | Inspect PR merge-revision settings, source branch, selected commit, and checkout log. |
| Template published but new steps are absent | Inspect approved adoption and the effective configuration for the specific app. |
| New template needs a missing secret or input | Ask the template owner to confirm protected provisioning and runtime input mapping. |
| New build succeeded but testers see the old version | Inspect whether delivery ran, the selected destination, processing/signing, audience assignment, and exact-version access. |

